.\" @(#)nfsd.8c 1.4 85/04/05 SMI;
.TH NFSD 8C "1 February 1985"
.SH NAME
nfsd, biod \- NFS daemons
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/nfsd
[ nservers ]
.sp .5
.B /etc/biod
[ nservers ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX  "nfsd command"  ""  "\fLnfsd\fP \(em NFS daemon"
.IX  "biod command"  ""  "\fLbiod\fP \(em NFS daemon"
.IX  servers  nfsd  ""  "\fLnfsd\fP \(em NFS daemon"
.IX  servers  biod  ""  "\fLbiod\fP \(em NFS daemon"
.I Nfsd
starts the 
.IR nfs (4)
server daemons that handle client filesystem requests.
.I Nservers
is the number of file system request daemons to start.
This number should be based on the load expected on this server;
four is a good number.
If
.I nservers
is not specified it defaults to one.
.PP
.I Biod
starts
.I nservers
asynchronous block I/O daemons.
This command is used on a NFS client to handle read-ahead and write-behind
of buffer cached blocks.
A good value for
.I nservers
is four; if not specified it defaults to one.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mountd(8c), exports(5)
